| Requirement | Why |
|-------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.4** (or any recent 2.x release) | The `sky` layer type and `setFog()` were added in v2.4. |
| **A DEM source** (e.g. Mapbox Terrain‑RGB, Open‑Topography, or any raster‑DEM you host) | Gives you the 3‑D terrain. |
| **`antialias: true`** when you create the map | The sky is rendered with smooth shading. |
| **A style that contains a `sky` layer** (you can add it to an existing style) | The sky is just another layer, placed **after** the terrain source and **before** any label/overlay layers. |

---

## 2. Minimal HTML / JavaScript example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- Base vector tiles (any style you like) ----
        "osm": {
          type: "vector",
          url: "https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/gl/voyager-gl-style/style.json"
        },
        // ---- DEM source (Terrain‑RGB) -----------------
        "terrain": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          url: "https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/tiles.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY",
          tileSize: 512,
          maxzoom: 14
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // ---- Base map (you can replace this with any style) ----
        {
          id: "background",
          type: "background",
          paint: { "background-color": "#e0e0e0" }
        },
        {
          id: "land",
          type: "fill",
          source: "osm",
          "source-layer": "landcover",
          paint: { "fill-color": "#e5e5e5" }
        },
        // ---- (more layers from your style would go here) ----
        // ---- SKY layer – **must be after terrain** ----
        {
          id: "sky",
          type: "sky",
          paint: {
            // Choose one of the two options below (comment the other)

            // ── OPTION A: Simple gradient sky (sunset‑like) ──
            // "sky-type": "gradient",
            // "sky-gradient": [
            //   "interpolate",
            //   ["linear"],
            //   ["sky-radial-progress"],
            //   0,   "rgba(135,206,235,1)",   // zenith – bright blue
            //   0.5, "rgba(135,206,235,0.5)", // mid‑sky – semi‑transparent
            //   1,   "rgba(255,255,255,0)"   // horizon – fade to transparent
            // ],
            // "sky-gradient-center": [0, 0],
            // "sky-gradient-radius": 90,
            // "sky-opacity": 0.9,

            // ── OPTION B: Real‑world atmosphere (recommended) ──
            "sky-type": "atmosphere",
            "sky-atmosphere-sun": [0.0, 0.0],          // azimuth, altitude (0‑1)
            "sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity": 15,       // brighter = sharper horizon
            "sky-atmosphere-color": "#88ccee",        // day‑sky colour
            "sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend": 0.5       // how quickly it fades to fog
          }
        }
      ]
    },

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Camera settings
    // -------------------------------------------------
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (any lon/lat works)
    zoom: 13,
    pitch: 70,                         // <-- you already had this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true,                   // important for smooth sky
    hash: true
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Enable the terrain
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add fog (haze) – this is what blends ground → sky
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setFog({
      // The colour of the fog at the horizon (usually matches sky colour)
      "color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.8)",
      // The colour of the fog high up (near the zenith)
      "high-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0)",
      // How far from the camera the fog starts / ends (in metres)
      // Adjust these numbers to make the haze thicker or thinner.
      "range": [0.5, 12],
      // Optional: make the fog a little more “space‑like” at the top
      "space-color": "#cceeff",
      // Optional: add a faint star field (0‑1)
      "star-intensity": 0.0
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Code | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| **Create the map** | `new maplibregl.Map({ antialias:true, pitch:70 })` | Enables smooth shading for the sky and terrain. |
| **Add a DEM source** | `"terrain"` source of type `raster-dem` | Supplies elevation data for 3‑D terrain. |
| **Enable terrain** | `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain', exaggeration:1.5})` | Lifts the ground into 3‑D. |
| **Add a `sky` layer** | Layer with `type: "sky"` | Renders a full‑screen sky that moves with the camera. Two ready‑made presets are shown: a simple gradient or a realistic atmosphere. |
| **Add fog** | `map.setFog({ … })` | Creates the “haze” that blends the terrain into the sky near the horizon. The `range` values control how close/far the fog starts and ends. |
| **Order matters** | `sky` layer is placed **after** the terrain source and **before** any label/overlay layers. | Guarantees the sky is drawn behind everything else but in front of the default page background. |

---

## 3. Tweaking the look

### 3.1. Sky type

| Property | Values | Typical use |
|----------|--------|-------------|
| `sky-type` | `"gradient"` | Simple two‑tone sky (good for stylised maps). |
| `sky-type` | `"atmosphere"` | Real‑world atmospheric scattering (default for most modern maps). |
| `sky-atmosphere-sun` | `[azimuth, altitude]` (0‑1) | Move the “sun” around; altitude = 0 is at the horizon, 1 is directly overhead. |
| `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity` | `0‑∞` | Higher = sharper, more contrast at the horizon. |
| `sky-atmosphere-color` | CSS colour | Base colour of the sky (e.g. `#88ccee` for a clear blue). |
| `sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend` | `0‑1` | How quickly the sky colour fades into the fog. Larger = smoother transition. |

**Quick tip:** If you want a sunset feel, set `sky-atmosphere-color` to a warm orange (`#ffb380`) and lower `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity` to ~5.

### 3.2. Fog (haze)

| Property | Typical values | What it changes |
|----------|----------------|-----------------|
| `color` | `"rgba(255,255,255,0.8)"` | Fog colour at the horizon (usually matches the sky). |
| `high-color` | `"rgba(255,255,255,0)"` | Fog colour near the zenith (transparent = no fog high up). |
| `range` | `[0.5, 12]` (meters) | Distance from the camera where fog starts (`0.5`) and ends (`12`). Smaller end‑range → thicker fog. |
| `space-color` | `"#cceeff"` | Colour of the “space” above the fog; useful for night‑time styles. |
| `star-intensity` | `0‑1` | Adds a faint star field when `sky-type` is `"atmosphere"` and you’re looking up. |

**Experiment:**  
- **Thick haze** → `range: [0.2, 5]`  
- **Thin haze** → `range: [1, 20]`  

### 3.3. Interaction with pitch & bearing

The sky automatically rotates with the camera, so when you pitch to 70° you’ll see the horizon line move up and the sky fill the rest of the canvas. The fog’s `range` is measured **in world units**, not screen pixels, so it stays consistent no matter how far you zoom.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Flat white background above the horizon** | No `sky` layer, or the layer is placed *before* the terrain source. | Ensure the `sky` layer is **after** the terrain source and **after** any `background` layer. |
| **Sky looks jagged / pixelated** | `antialias: false` (default for older MapLibre builds). | Set `antialias: true` when creating the map. |
| **Fog never appears** | `setFog()` called **before** the map is loaded, or `range` values are too far away. | Call `map.setFog()` inside `map.on('load', …)` and use modest

Thinking: The user has a pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap as JPEG raster tiles, wants to put it under vector data layers with labels still on top. They ask "How do I add it?" Likely they are using some GIS software or web mapping library (e.g., Leaflet, OpenLayers, Mapbox GL, ArcGIS). Need to ask clarifying which platform. Provide general steps: add as raster tile layer, set z-index lower than vector layers, ensure labels are separate layer on top. For Leaflet: L.tileLayer(urlTemplate, {maxZoom, attribution}).addTo(map); then add vector layers after, or set pane with lower z-index. For OpenLayers: ol.layer.Tile with source XYZ, set zIndex. For Mapbox GL: addSource with type 'raster', addLayer with type 'raster', before label layer. Also ensure tile size, projection (Web Mercator). Provide code snippets. Also mention handling retina, tile size, caching, cross-origin. Provide tips for labeling: use separate label layer from vector source, or use Mapbox style. Also mention using WMTS if needed. Provide steps for QGIS: add raster tile layer via XYZ Tiles, set layer order.

Thus answer: ask which platform, then give generic instructions and examples.

Below is a **step‑by‑step cheat‑sheet** you can copy‑paste into the most common mapping stacks (Leaflet, OpenLayers, Mapbox GL JS, and QGIS Desktop).  
All of them do the same thing:

1. **Add the JPEG tile service as a raster tile layer** (XYZ/WMTS).  
2. **Give it a low z‑index / put it in a “basemap” pane** so it sits underneath everything else.  
3. **Add your vector layers** (GeoJSON, VectorTile, WMS, etc.) **after** the raster, or explicitly set a higher z‑index.  
4. **Add a separate label layer** (or keep the labels that are already part of your vector data) and make sure its z‑index is the highest.

---

## 1️⃣  Identify the tile URL pattern

Your provider should give you a URL template that looks like one of these:

| Format | Example |
|--------|---------|
| XYZ (most common) | `https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg` |
| WMTS (with `TileMatrixSet`) | `https://tiles.example.com/wmts?layer=relief&style=default&tilematrixset=GoogleMapsCompatible&TileMatrix={z}&TileRow={y}&TileCol={x}` |
| TMS (origin bottom‑left) | `https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{-y}/{x}.jpg` |

> **Tip:** If you’re not sure, open the URL in a browser with a known zoom/xy (e.g. `.../0/0/0.jpg`). If you get a 404, try swapping `{x}` and `{y}` or using `{-y}` for TMS.

---

## 2️⃣  Add the raster basemap

### **Leaflet**

```js
// 1️⃣ Create a dedicated pane for the basemap (optional but clean)
map.createPane('basemap');
map.getPane('basemap').style.zIndex = 200;   // lower than vector panes (400+)

// 2️⃣ Add the JPEG tile layer
L.tileLayer('https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg', {
  pane: 'basemap',          // <-- forces it into the low‑z pane
  maxZoom: 19,
  attribution: '&copy; YourProvider',
  crossOrigin: true        // needed if you want to read pixel data later
}).addTo(map);
```

**Add your vector layers after the basemap** (or give them a higher `pane`/`zIndex`).

```js
// Example vector layer (GeoJSON)
L.geoJSON(myGeoJSON, {
  pane: 'overlay',         // default pane has zIndex 400
  style: {...}
}).addTo(map);

// Example label layer (if you have a separate tile set for labels)
L.tileLayer('https://tiles.example.com/labels/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  pane: 'labels',
  zIndex: 600,             // on top of everything
  opacity: 0.9
}).addTo(map);
```

---

### **OpenLayers (v7+)**

```js
import 'ol/ol.css';
import {Map, View} from 'ol';
import TileLayer from 'ol/layer/Tile';
import XYZ from 'ol/source/XYZ';
import VectorLayer from 'ol/layer/Vector';
import VectorSource from 'ol/source/Vector';
import {fromLonLat} from 'ol/proj';

// 1️⃣ Basemap raster layer
const reliefLayer = new TileLayer({
  source: new XYZ({
    url: 'https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg',
    crossOrigin: 'anonymous',
    maxZoom: 19
  }),
  zIndex: 0               // lowest
});

// 2️⃣ Your vector data
const vectorLayer = new VectorLayer({
  source: new VectorSource({
    url: 'data/my-features.geojson',
    format: new ol.format.GeoJSON()
  }),
  zIndex: 10
});

// 3️⃣ Optional label tile layer (if you have one)
const labelLayer = new TileLayer({
  source: new XYZ({
    url: 'https://tiles.example.com/labels/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    crossOrigin: 'anonymous'
  }),
  zIndex: 20
});

const map = new Map({
  target: 'map',
  layers: [reliefLayer, vectorLayer, labelLayer],
  view: new View({
    center: fromLonLat([0, 0]),
    zoom: 2,
    projection: 'EPSG:3857'   // Web Mercator – matches most XYZ tiles
  })
});
```

---

### **Mapbox GL JS (v2+)**

```js
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';

const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // 1️⃣ Raster basemap source
      'relief': {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: ['https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution: '© YourProvider',
        maxzoom: 19
      },
      // 2️⃣ Vector source (GeoJSON or vector tiles)
      'my-data': {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: 'data/my-features.geojson'
      },
      // 3️⃣ Optional label raster source
      'labels': {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: ['https://tiles.example.com/labels/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
        tileSize: 256,
        maxzoom: 19
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Basemap raster layer (lowest)
      {
        id: 'relief',
        type: 'raster',
        source: 'relief',
        paint: {}
      },
      // Your vector layer (on top of basemap)
      {
        id: 'my-data-fill',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'my-data',
        paint: {
          'fill-color': '#3388ff',
          'fill-opacity': 0.4
        }
      },
      // Optional label raster layer (topmost)
      {
        id: 'labels',
        type: 'raster',
        source: 'labels',
        paint: {}
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

*If you already have a style that includes a label layer (e.g., `mapbox://styles/...`), just insert the raster source **before** the label layer using `map.addSource` + `map.addLayer` with the `before: 'label-layer-id'` option.*

---

### **QGIS Desktop (≥ 3.28)**

1. **Add an “XYZ Tiles” connection**  
   *Menu → Browser → XYZ Tiles → New Connection*  
   - **Name:** `Relief Basemap`  
   - **URL:** `https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`  
   - **Max Zoom:** `19` (or whatever your provider supports)  
   - **Tile format:** `jpg` (or `png`)  

2. **Drag the new XYZ entry onto the map canvas** – it appears as a raster layer.

3. **Re‑order layers** in the Layers panel:  
   - Put **Relief Basemap** at the bottom.  
   - Add your vector layers (Shapefile, GeoPackage, PostGIS, etc.) **above** it.  
   - If you have a separate label tile set, add it **above** the vectors.

4. **Set “Layer Rendering Order”** (Project → Properties → Rendering) if you need fine‑grained control; otherwise the panel order is enough.

5. **Optional – make the raster transparent** (if you want to see underlying imagery):  
   - Right‑click the raster → Properties → Transparency → Add a “No data” value (e.g., 0) or adjust the global opacity slider.

---

## 3️⃣  Keep the labels on top

| Platform | How to guarantee label‑on‑top |
|----------|------------------------------|
| **Leaflet** | Put the label tile layer in a pane with a higher `zIndex` (`map.createPane('labels'); … zIndex: 600`). |
| **OpenLayers** | Set `zIndex` of the label `TileLayer` > `zIndex` of all vector layers. |
| **Mapbox GL** | Add the label layer **after** the vector layers, or use `before: 'my-data-fill'` when calling `addLayer`. |
| **QGIS** | Drag the label raster **above** the vector layers in the Layers panel. |

If your vector data already contains **text labels** (e.g., via `TextSymbolizer` in a style), they will automatically render on top of any raster basemap as long as the raster is placed lower in the layer stack.

---

## 4️⃣  Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them

| Issue | Why it Happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **Tiles appear shifted / mis‑aligned** | Tile service uses a different projection (e.g., EPSG:4326 or a custom TMS) while your map is in Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). | • Verify the provider’s projection. <br>• If it’s EPSG:4326, set `crs: L.CRS.EPSG4326` in Leaflet or `projection: 'EPSG:4326'` in OpenLayers. <br>• For Mapbox GL you must re‑project the source to Web Mercator (Mapbox only supports 3857). |
| **Tiles are blurry on high‑DPI screens** | Tiles are 256 px but the device pixel ratio is 2+. | • Use `detectRetina: true` in Leaflet. <br>• In OpenLayers, set `tilePixelRatio: window.devicePixelRatio`. <br>• In Mapbox GL, set `tileSize: 512` and `maxzoom` accordingly, or serve @2x tiles (`.../{z}/{x}/{y}@2x.jpg`). |
| **CORS errors** | The tile server doesn’t send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin` headers. | • Add `crossOrigin: 'anonymous'` (Leaflet/OpenLayers) or `crossOrigin: true` (Mapbox GL). <br>• If you control the server, enable CORS. <br>• As a last resort, proxy the tiles through your own domain. |
| **Labels disappear under vector fill** | Vector polygon fill is opaque and covers the label raster. | • Reduce polygon opacity (`fill-opacity` in Mapbox GL, `fillOpacity` in Leaflet). <br>• Or move the label raster **above** the polygon layer (higher `zIndex`). |
| **Too many HTTP requests** | Each tile is a separate request; many layers multiply the count. | • Use a **single combined raster** (relief + labels) if you can. <br>• Enable HTTP/2 on your server. <br>• Use a CDN with caching. |
| **Tile grid mismatch (e.g., 0/0/0 missing)** | Provider starts at zoom 1 or uses a different tile origin. | • Adjust the URL template (`{z+1}` or `{y}` → `{-y-1}`) or set `tms: true` in Leaflet. <br>• In OpenLayers, set `tileGrid: ol.tilegrid.createXYZ({maxZoom: 19, tileSize: 256, extent: ...})`. |

---

## 5️⃣  Quick “Copy‑Paste” Summary

Below are the **minimal snippets** you can drop into a fresh HTML page (replace the URL with yours).

### Leaflet (HTML)

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9/dist/leaflet.css"/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script>
  const map = L.map('map').setView([0,0], 2);

  // Basemap pane (low z-index)
  map.createPane('basemap');
  map.getPane('basemap').style.zIndex = 200;

  // Relief raster
  L.tileLayer('https://tiles.example.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg', {
    pane: 'basemap',
    maxZoom: 19,
    attribution: '&copy; YourProvider',
    crossOrigin: true
  }).addTo(map);

  // Example vector (GeoJSON)
  L.geoJSON(myGeoJSON, {style:{color:'#ff6600', weight:2}}
